    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born into a slave-owning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> family, Jefferson drafted the Declaration of Independence in June 1776 and went on to serve as governor, minister to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, secretary of state, vice president and, from 1801 to 1809, president.</li><li>As president he doubled the size of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a>, sent Lewis and Clark to the Pacific and reduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-debt">national debt</a> from $83 million to $57 million, but his Embargo Act of 1807 brought economic chaos.</li><li>He wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was harmful to slave and master, yet he enslaved about 600 people over his lifetime, and scholars give sharply differing readings of his views and conduct.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Jefferson's first library, of about 200 volumes, was destroyed in a fire at Shadwell in 1770; in 1814 he sold his second library, nearly 6,500 volumes, to the U.S. government for $23,950 after British forces burned the Library of Congress.</p></aside>
    </section>

